<p>For years, the web’s ambition was to get bigger. More people. More pages. More reach. Every product wanted to become a platform; every platform wanted to become a city. Lately I’ve been wondering what we traded away for all that scale.</p>

<p>I don’t mean smaller in the nostalgic sense. I’m not pining for hand-coded homepages, hit counters, and guestbooks. I mean smaller in the way a good room is small: specific, comfortable, and sized for the people who actually gather there.</p>

<h2>A room, not a stadium</h2>

<p>The small web is already here. It’s a forum with forty regulars. A newsletter written for six hundred people who know why they subscribed. A personal site that exists for no commercial reason at all. These places don’t try to capture the whole world. They try to be useful to someone.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>The best places online feel less like broadcasts and more like rooms with the door left open.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>There is a different set of decisions available when you stop optimizing for maximum reach. You can keep the rough edges. You can write the long version. You can let the archive grow. You can make something that rewards returning, instead of something that has to be endlessly refilled to stay alive.</p>

<p>Scale is not a virtue by itself. Sometimes it’s just the thing that makes a place impossible to keep clean. I’d like to spend more of my time in rooms. I’d like to build a few of my own.</p>
